{"answer":"When a fisher’s catch exceeds 1 unit, the excess is first entered into the communal ledger as a surplus entry. The surplus is not redistributed immediately during the fishing session; instead, it is held until the end of that fishing day when the rotating observer reviews the ledger. At that point, the surplus is divided equally among all villagers who, at the end of the day, have not yet reached the 1‑unit threshold. If, after the day’s fishing, every villager has already reached 1 unit, there are no recipients for the surplus. In that case the surplus remains in a communal reserve fund, to be drawn upon later when a villager falls below the threshold or for community‑wide needs.

This means that redistribution is a post‑fishing, end‑of‑day event rather than a real‑time adjustment during fishing.", "revised_policy":"(no community policy has been adopted yet)

"Each villager may take up to 1 unit of fish per day; any catch beyond that is logged in a communal ledger as surplus. At the end of each fishing day, the rotating observer reviews the ledger and, if any surplus exists, redistributes it equally among all villagers who have not yet reached 1 unit by the end of that day. If no villager is below 1 unit, the surplus is stored in a communal reserve for future use. All villagers meet at the dock at the start of the day to declare intended catch. When a villager reaches 1 unit, any further attempts are recorded in the ledger but the fisher may still fish, with excess noted. The rotating observer, drawn by hat, inspects the ledger and watches fishing to enforce compliance. While acting as the observer, that villager must not fish during the observation window, but may fish before or after the observation period."", "revised_operationalization":"At day start, all villagers declare intended catch. The observer monitors fishing. If a fisher exceeds 1 unit, the surplus is recorded in the ledger. No immediate redistribution occurs. At the close of the day, the observer reviews the ledger, identifies any surplus, and calculates the number of villagers who have <1 unit at day’s end. Surplus is divided equally among those villagers. If there are none, surplus is moved to the communal reserve. The reserve can be used when a villager later falls below 1 unit or for other community purposes. The observer remains inactive during the observation window but can fish before or after that window.", "reasoning":"I answered that redistribution occurs at day’s end to ensure fairness and clear record‑keeping, and that surplus is kept in reserve when there are no eligible recipients, to avoid wasting fish and to provide a buffer for future fishing days."}

