{"answer":"A month is the 30‑day cycle that begins on the first day of our communal lunar calendar. All violations that occur within those 30 days are counted toward the skip‑day or fine thresholds. After the 30th day, the count resets.

The policy itself remains unchanged; I am merely adding the definition of a month to avoid confusion.","revised_policy":"Policy: Each fisher may take no more than three units per trip and must leave at least one unit in the lake; any excess is returned immediately. A month for tallying violations is the 30‑day cycle that begins on the first day of our communal lunar calendar.

Operationalization: After each trip, the fisher records units caught in a personal ledger and reports to the observer before sunset. The observer tallies all catches in the communal ledger, and the lake keeper with the council counts remaining fish using a calibrated net. If a fisher exceeds the 3‑unit limit or the lake drops below one unit, the excess fish is returned to the lake, and the fisher is flagged. Two consecutive violations or three separate violations in a month trigger a skip‑day imposed by a majority vote of the five elders (at least three votes). Repeated violations incur a fine set annually by the Community Council, deducted from the fisher’s next communal contribution. All records are kept in the communal ledger, reviewed nightly, and the policy is enforced by the lake keeper and council.","revised_operationalization":"After each trip, the fisher records units caught in a personal ledger and reports to the observer before sunset. The observer tallies all catches in the communal ledger, and the lake keeper with the council counts remaining fish using a calibrated net. If a fisher exceeds the 3‑unit limit or the lake drops below one unit, the excess fish is returned to the lake, and the fisher is flagged. Two consecutive violations or three separate violations in a month trigger a skip‑day imposed by a majority vote of the five elders (at least three votes). Repeated violations incur a fine set annually by the Community Council, deducted from the fisher’s next communal contribution. All records are kept in the communal ledger, reviewed nightly, and the policy is enforced by the lake keeper and council.","reasoning":"I added the definition of a month to eliminate ambiguity, as the community may otherwise interpret it as a calendar month or a 30‑day period. Clarifying it to a 30‑day lunar cycle ensures everyone tracks violations consistently. The rest of the policy and operational details stay the same because they already cover the mechanics of catching, reporting, and enforcement."}

